# Curated CA trust anchors for dial-idf embedded TLS verification.
#
# Why curated instead of the full esp_crt_bundle: on this IDF v6.0 toolchain
# build, esp_crt_bundle is broken, so we embed a small set of PEM roots
# directly (EMBED_TXTFILES, exposed via dial_oauth_root_ca()) and verify
# against them with mbedtls. mbedtls parses a concatenated PEM file into a
# list of independent trust anchors, so this file is simply the roots below
# back to back -- lines outside a BEGIN/END block (like this header and the
# one-line labels before each cert) are ignored by the PEM parser.
#
# Roots included and why:
#   - GlobalSign Root CA (R1) + GTS Root R4: current Orion (mcp.orionsleep.com)
#     chain as observed live.
#   - GTS Root R1: Google Trust Services' primary RSA root, kept alongside
#     R4 as headroom in case Orion's chain is re-issued under it.
#   - ISRG Root X1: Let's Encrypt, common for self-hosted/small-vendor APIs.
#   - USERTrust RSA + USERTrust ECC Certification Authority: Sectigo, the
#     github.com chain.
#   - DigiCert Global Root CA + DigiCert Global Root G2: GitHub's assets/
#     objects CDN (objects.githubusercontent.com) has historically chained
#     through DigiCert.
#   - Amazon Root CA 1: AWS-hosted APIs.
#
# This set exists to survive a routine Orion CA rotation without bricking
# device provisioning/control, and to support planned OTA-from-GitHub
# (github.com + objects.githubusercontent.com) without another firmware
# push. Extracted from the macOS Homebrew OpenSSL trust store
# (/opt/homebrew/etc/openssl@3/cert.pem) on 2026-07-09; each cert verified
# individually (openssl x509 -noout -subject -enddate) and the resulting
# chains verified live against mcp.orionsleep.com, github.com, and
# objects.githubusercontent.com.
#
# Widened 2026-07-27 for unattended longevity (this device may outlive its
# maintainer; a stale anchor set is the one failure OTA cannot heal, because
# OTA needs TLS too). Nine anchors added, each verified by tying it
# cryptographically to a root already in this file (SPKI match against the
# CA's official repository copy, or signature check of the CA's published
# cross-sign) -- never trusted on the strength of a download alone:
#   - GTS Root R2 + R3 (2036): rest of the Google family Orion chains under;
#     pki.goog roots.pem authenticated by SPKI match of our existing R1/R4.
#   - ISRG Root X2 (2040), Root YR + Root YE (2045): Let's Encrypt successor
#     generations -- Root YR is the root actually serving
#     objects.githubusercontent.com (OTA downloads) today, previously covered
#     only via its X1 cross-sign, which Let's Encrypt will someday retire.
#     Authenticated via the published *-by-x1/x2 cross-signs against our X1.
#   - Sectigo Public Server Authentication Root E46 (cross-signed form, 2038):
#     modern Sectigo root above github.com's chain; signature verified
#     against our USERTrust ECC.
#   - Amazon Root CA 2-4 (2040): amazontrust.com repository authenticated by
#     SPKI match of our existing Amazon Root CA 1.
# Earliest LOAD-BEARING expiry after this widening: DigiCert Global Root CA
# 2031 (GlobalSign R1's Jan 2028 expiry is inert -- it only ever appeared as
# GTS R4's cross-sign, which mbedtls ignores because R4 itself is here).
# CI (.github/workflows/certs.yml) probes the live hosts against this file
# monthly and fails when any anchor nears expiry.
# Revisit esp_crt_bundle (the full Mozilla set) if the pinned IDF ever moves
# past v6.0, where its matching is broken.

